---
import {
  Alert02Icon,
  Analytics01Icon,
  McpServerIcon,
  Route01Icon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo index', text: "Google's indexing reasons and pages that lost their clicks.", href: '/docs/indexing', icon: Alert02Icon },
  { title: 'seo crawl', text: 'Crawl the site from your machine before and after a release.', href: '/docs/crawl', icon: Route01Icon },
  { title: 'seo report', text: 'Search Console sections, including sudden traffic drops.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Analytics01Icon },
  { title: 'Hosted MCP', text: 'Connect seoskill.dev/mcp and sign in with Google.', href: '/docs/mcp', icon: McpServerIcon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="SEO monitoring for AI agents"
  description="SEO monitoring for your own site: a weekly crawl, daily Google index checks and alerts when a page drops out of the index, plus free checks your agent runs when you ask."
  path="/features/seo-monitoring"
  lead="Find out when a page drops out of Google, a release breaks titles or links, or clicks fall away, from what Google and your own site report."
  {related}
>
  <h2>Two ways to monitor a site</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Free tools</th><th>Weekly site audit</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Who runs it</td><td>Your agent, when you ask or on a schedule you set.</td><td>Our servers, in the background.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Site crawl</td><td>On your machine with the CLI.</td><td>Every week, with what is new, back or resolved since the crawl before.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google index</td><td>100 known URLs a run on the hosted MCP, more from the CLI, each checked live.</td><td>Up to the property's full 2,000 URL Inspection checks a day, top pages first.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alerts</td><td>None. Your agent reports what it finds.</td><td>Email and webhook when a page drops out of the index or comes back.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>History</td><td>Your agent keeps each result.</td><td>Kept on the server for each audited site.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Free, with no usage cap.</td><td>$50 a month per site.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>SEO alerts when a page leaves Google's index</h2>
  <p>
    Sites in the weekly site audit get alerts without asking. You get one
    when a page drops out of Google's index or comes back. A top page's drop
    is sent only after a second check agrees, so one odd answer from Google
    does not wake you up. URL Inspection shows Google's latest indexing
    decision, and search results can take a few days to catch up, so each
    dropped page has a one-click site: search to see what Google shows
    right now. Each run of a site's background checks that finds
    changes sends one alert for that site. When Google gives the reason{' '}
    <a href="/features/crawled-currently-not-indexed">crawled, currently not
    indexed</a>, it read the page and chose to leave it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each alert type has its own email and webhook switch, under Alerts on
    your <a href="/account">account
    page</a>. Both are on by default, and the webhook starts once you save a
    URL. Email goes to your first linked Google account, and every email
    has a link that turns that type of email off.
    Webhooks are signed the <a href="https://www.standardwebhooks.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Standard Webhooks</a> way.
    After a timeout or a server error, a webhook is tried again up to 6
    times over about a day.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Monday morning a weekly summary covers every audited site: Google
    index checks, what the weekly crawl found and search clicks against the
    week before.
  </p>

  <h2>What the weekly crawl watches</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Internal links to errors, redirects and pages that could not be fetched.</li>
    <li>Missing or duplicate titles, missing descriptions and missing H1s.</li>
    <li>Pages that ask not to be indexed, and canonicals that point to another URL.</li>
    <li>Sitemap URLs that do not return 200.</li>
    <li>Thin pages under 100 words (a heuristic, not a Google rule).</li>
    <li>Pages where JavaScript changes the title, canonical or robots tag.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each finding says whether it is new, back or resolved since the crawl
    before, so a release that broke something stands out from the problems
    you already knew about.
  </p>

  <h2>Start the weekly site audit</h2>
  <p>
    Pick a Search Console property on the Sites tab of your account page,
    or ask your agent:
  </p>
  <pre><code>Add example.com to the weekly site audit</code></pre>
  <p>
    Your first site opens Stripe Checkout. Your agent can give you that link,
    but it cannot pay for you. The first crawl and index checks start once
    the plan is active, and you can cancel any time.
  </p>

  <h2>Monitor with the free tools instead</h2>
  <p>
    Run the index check once for a baseline and keep the full result. Run it
    again each week with the same number of pages and compare the two by
    page and reason. A new reason is the thing to look at.
    The <code>seo</code> command keeps no history here, so a scheduled agent task that
    saves each week's result works well.
  </p>
  <pre><code>seo index --site example.com --detail full</code></pre>
  <p>
    After a release, crawl the site and compare it with the crawl from
    before, and run <a href="/docs/compare">seo compare</a> on the release
    date once a week or two of Search Console data has come in. The report's
    traffic drop section looks for a sudden change in daily clicks that
    lasted, with the date it started.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI answers are not part of the weekly audit. To follow them, run the
    same questions through the{' '}
    <a href="/features/ai-visibility-checker">AI visibility checker</a> once
    a month and compare the two results.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
